Mojo native CPU port
===================
Development port: lang/mojo. Release qualification remains incomplete.
Recorded on 2026-10-09; native amd64 FreeBSD 16 / 5BSD.

Source and toolchain
--------------------
Upstream: https://github.com/modular/modular
Revision: babedf564bd7e128bd161453f341f6b4b61aa3ca
Mojo version: 1.2.0.dev2026100805
Ports version: 1.2.0.d2026100805
Licenses: Apache-2.0 with LLVM exceptions; formatter also includes MIT and PSFL components.
This is a development snapshot, not a qualified stable release.

The compiler uses upstream's pinned LLVM/MLIR revision
 a83a3a4ebd64653dbe6a85819996b3bcfd6753ec (24.0.0git), archive SHA256
 c26cddd8981c07cd562d05ef2baa58c2b30e0dd3537b8c42971463680d54dc1f,
with upstream's LLVM patches. Keep this toolchain private to Mojo. The
native compiler builds with existing base Clang 21.1.8; no base compiler,
kernel or libc changes were required. Base LLVM upgrades are separate work.
Base MLIR integration was not found; devel/llvm21 enables MLIR by default.

The build dependency is devel/bazel9 >= 9.2.0. The native Bazel 9.2.0_4
executable and OpenJDK 21.0.12+8.2_2 packages now build. Bazel passes stage QA
and plist checks; the rebuilt JDK passes compilation, virtual-thread,
subprocess and file-I/O checks. Native Bazel passes the new NUMA/affinity
tests and the full CPU rerun (1169 pass, six skip). The fresh end-to-end ports
build is still in progress;
clean Bazel/JDK installation still needs native dependency qualification.
Earlier validation used the Java-only bootstrap. Standalone build/test
actions avoid depending on a native sandbox helper.

Port contents and dependencies
------------------------------
The port fetches 503 checksummed distfiles during the fetch phase, including
all required Bazel registry files, dependency archives, patches and overlays.
Its build and test commands disable repository downloads and freeze the
Bazel lockfile. The native compiler, runtime and stdlib are built from source
with all logical CPUs. Runtime dependencies are curl, Python 3.12 and the
formatter's click, mypy_extensions, pathspec, platformdirs and tomli Python ports;
Bazel and LLVM are not package runtime dependencies. Tests also use NumPy and pytest.

The package installs a private SDK in libexec/mojo, a bin/mojo launcher,
the language server, formatter, Python importer, documentation and
operators/process/Python examples.
The launcher selects the SDK, system linker and Python interpreter. ELF
RUNPATHs use the private SDK; CompilerRT has a stable library SONAME.
The default launcher disables telemetry and unsupported crash reporting.

Only amd64 FreeBSD 16 has been exercised; Mojo programs were tested with
the x86-64-v3 CPU configuration. The patch includes an aarch64 target triple,
but that architecture is not enabled in the port without qualification.
GPU execution, MAX, debugger and REPL are not included. Native NUMA CPU
discovery and thread CPU/memory affinity pass on this single-domain host and
in a two-domain CPU-only VM. Physical multi-socket placement, PCI locality and
broader socket bindings remain unqualified.
LLVM's optional LZMA support is disabled pending native dependency wiring.

FreeBSD integration
-------------------
The downstream source patch selects native platform triples, Python and
system curl, and fixes dependency build assumptions for FreeBSD. Runtime
changes include arc4random_buf, aligned allocation, sysctl host information,
x86 CPUID cache information, ELF symbol resolution and GNU build IDs.
The physical-core query uses kern.smp.cores instead of LLVM's unimplemented
FreeBSD query. CompilerRT's SONAME and Python package discovery support the
installed SDK layout.

Standard-library changes cover platform detection, errno/open/socket/clock
constants, RTLD_DEFAULT and native stat/dirent/passwd layouts. Measured
amd64 sizes are stat=224, dirent=280 (name offset 24), passwd=80, pid_t=4.
The timestamp assertion accepts a same-width signed integral representation;
its units and conversion behavior are unchanged. Boundary probes passed.
Other fixes address native Python toolchain fields, C integer optionals,
Span equality and a formatting-test module-name collision.

Test configuration advertises only features actually compiled into native
tools. Optimized builds lack assertion-only LLVM debug options. Tests retain
functional RUN lines when only a debug-output check needs ASSERTIONS.
CPU tests do not acquire unnecessary MAX dependencies. Fix-it tests operate
on temporary copies, and the bad-symlink parser test cleans its temporary
directory before reruns. The object/shared-library integration tests also
clear their own temporary directories before creation, so standalone reruns
do not fail on stale output. These are tool/test changes, not OS changes.

The MLIR timing report now gives sequential pass managers separate hidden
parent scopes; reused pass addresses no longer hide later pass names. The
existing timing regression passes unchanged. The private LLVM patch preserves
ASan signal handlers installed through FreeBSD's raw syscall before libthr
initializes its sigaction cache. Synchronizing the cached action before LLVM
registers its own handler fixes both crash tracing and configuration-file
cleanup. No base LLVM or libc changes are involved.

Validation and outstanding work
-------------------------------
The combined coverage-audit run passed 1169 Bazel test targets, with
6 skipped targets and no failures (205 more passing targets than the
original CPU selection). The port's Makefile.tests now includes the additional
native targets: all 35 applicable Support unit suites, all eight AsyncRT unit
suites, 155 documentation examples, documentation compiler and Support
regressions, the build-ID shared-library test, and small example/tool tests.
FreeBSD OS detection and socket layouts have new native tests. The 96 CLI cases
and 20 documentation-generator cases pass; the 208 integration cases have
207 passes and one expected failure. See README.mojo-tests.5BSD for remaining
coverage gaps, including skips inside passing targets and missing AI runtimes.

An additional staged-package Python run passes 93 cases with no skips or
failures: eight importer/path cases and 85 cases across all 12 extension
example groups. These are separate from the 1169 Bazel target count.
The reusable target is make test-python-package in lang/mojo. It builds
upstream extension fixtures with the staged compiler and runs their original
pytest checks. The verified environment used Python 3.12, pytest 8.2.2 and
NumPy 2.4.6; newer pytest versions selected by ports are not yet qualified.
The launcher imports sys into __main__, as the upstream launcher does, before
pytest starts; omitting that setup caused earlier fixture failures. Original
failure logs and the corrected results are retained under
/usr/obj/mojo-port-validation/2026-10-09/python-package-target/ and
python-harness/. Bazel's FreeBSD pycross dependency selection is still open.

The refreshed package passed stage QA, plist checks, real packaged examples,
Python interop, native CPU-count checks and both ASan crash-trace modes.
Package SHA256: e67115eac65dbb643d21ea5dc7f926a86ad9a1cbcc310f9eb55e01f70530a5df
Evidence: /usr/obj/mojo-port-validation/2026-10-09/coverage-audit/.
Hardware targets and runtime blockers: README.mojo-hardware.5BSD.

Earlier CPU baseline
--------------------
The earlier expanded CPU run completed with 964 passing Bazel targets,
6 skipped targets and no failed targets. This includes compiler/support
unit tests, all 382 parser targets, KGEN, the standard-library CPU selection,
the CLI group, compiler integration, stdlib-source determinism, and examples.
The earlier 966-target run also passed (958 passed, eight skipped).
The CPU CLI and integration aggregates run exclusively after other build/test
work because lit uses all host CPUs. CLI and stdlib-source determinism use
the large timeout; a combined run exposed contention and timeouts under the
default size. Determinism imports the whole standard library four times.

The 85-case CLI group has 84 passes and one Linux-only unsupported case.
The 208-case compiler integration group has 206 passes, one expected failure
and one Linux-only unsupported case. Both crash regressions pass with and
without Mojo tracing, including ASan diagnostics and source locations. The
erf/tanh math suites execute on FreeBSD and pass all eight cases.

Across the final run, the audit records 972 lit passes,
2 expected failures and 8 unsupported cases. Mojo TestSuite
summaries report 2532 passing cases and three intentional filtering-test skips.
These case totals are a different level of counting from Bazel target totals.

The six skipped Bazel targets are the GPU range-codegen test, Linux/macOS
network ABI tests, and aarch64/Linux/macOS platform tests. Three additional
GPU-only stdlib targets are explicitly excluded by the CPU recipe. Lit skips
cover foreign platforms and LLVM assertion-only debug features unavailable
in the optimized build. Unsupported and expected-failure cases are not passes.
The CPU integration group excludes eight MAX-dependent layout files; GPU/MAX,
debugger and full upstream product qualification are outside this port.

The complete source patch applies to a fresh upstream archive using the ports
framework's patch arguments. Ports fetch/checksum/extract/patch and fresh
dependency analysis with downloads disabled and a frozen lockfile passed.
These checks are not a fresh complete offline compilation.

Ports stage-qa, check-plist and package creation passed using already-built
native artifacts and a local chrpath bootstrap. The refreshed package is:
 /usr/obj/mojo-port-stage-check/pkg/mojo-1.2.0.d2026100805.pkg
A fresh unpack compiled and ran operators/process and Python hello/Person;
all ten packaged operator cases passed. CPU counts match the kernel (8 physical,
16 logical). Packaged ASan crash diagnostics passed with tracing enabled and
disabled. Package ABI: FreeBSD:16:amd64; runtime dependencies are curl/Python3.12.

Still required before release qualification: a clean full ports build with
the packaged Bazel, and clean-VM package installation, dependency resolution,
offline examples, removal and reinstall. Native Bazel packaging is complete;
its isolated offline build-action and package lifecycle qualification now passes. No host installation, system upgrade, reboot or update-repository
publication was performed.

